|Feature scaling: Normalization vs standardization|


Suppose a model receives two features:
- Age: 25
- Annual income: 600,000
Income has much larger numerical values than age. Some algorithms can be sensitive to these differences.

A. Min-Max normalization
Transforms values to a specified range, often 0 to 1.
\[
x'=\frac{x-x_{\min}}{x_{\max}-x_{\min}}
\]

If the minimum age is 20 and the maximum is 60, an age of 40 becomes:

\[
\frac{40-20}{60-20}=0.5
\]



B. Standardization
Transforms a value using the mean and standard deviation.
\[
z=\frac{x-\mu}{\sigma}
\]

Here, \(\mu\) is the mean and \(\sigma\) is the standard deviation.

A standardized value indicates how far a data point is from the training mean in standard-deviation units.
