Q1. What is data preprocessing?
It is the process of cleaning and transforming raw data into a suitable format for a machine learning model. It can include missing-value handling, encoding, scaling, outlier treatment, and feature engineering.
Q2. What is the difference between normalization and standardization?
Normalization commonly rescales values to a fixed range such as 0–1. Standardization subtracts the mean and divides by the standard deviation.
Q3. Why do we use one-hot encoding?
It converts nominal categories into binary columns without imposing an artificial numerical order on the categories.
Q4. Why should we fit a scaler only on training data?
To prevent information from the test set influencing the learned preprocessing parameters. We fit on training data and use the same fitted transformation on test and production data.
Q5. How do you handle outliers?
First investigate whether they are data errors or valid observations. Depending on the context, I may correct, retain, cap, transform, or use robust methods. I avoid deleting outliers automatically.
Q6. What is the benefit of a Scikit-learn Pipeline?
It combines preprocessing and modeling into a repeatable workflow, reduces inconsistent transformations, and helps prevent leakage when trained correctly.
Q7. How is feature engineering useful in banking?
It uses domain knowledge to create informative variables, such as debt-to-income ratio, account age, and recent missed-payment count, which may help a model identify patterns relevant to default risk.
